B.bqo=new A.ll('This card uses the "Return on cost" lens.\n\nReturn on cost = your profit \xf7 the cost of what you currently hold. It answers: "how far up are my holdings right now?"\n\nThat is why it can differ from the "Total return" shown in the "Do I Beat the Market?" card. Both are correct \u2014 they just divide by different things:\n\n\u2022 Return on cost (here) \u2192 divides by the cost of your current holdings.\n\u2022 Total return (money-weighted) \u2192 divides by every dollar you ever invested, and adds back the cash from sells and dividends.\n\nIRR is the same profit expressed as an annual rate, adjusted for when you invested.',null)
B.bqp=new A.ll('The "You" row uses the "Total return (money-weighted)" lens.\n\nIt divides your total profit by every dollar you have ever invested, and counts the cash you got back from sells and dividends as part of your return.\n\nThis is usually different from the "Return on cost" shown on the "Real vs Paper" card \u2014 and both are correct. They look at your performance through different lenses:\n\n\u2022 Total return (here) \u2192 divides by all money ever invested, and rewards realised gains and dividends.\n\u2022 Return on cost (Real vs Paper) \u2192 divides by the cost of only what you still hold.\n\nEvery target on the leaderboard is measured with this same total-return lens \u2014 including its own dividends, counted as cash exactly like yours \u2014 so the comparison is fair.',null)
